# RTL layout

## Overview

Direction is set once, on the root, and everything else follows it. The hard bugs are not margins. They are left-to-right runs (phone numbers, IBANs, codes, emails) inside Arabic text, which the bidi algorithm reorders. This skill sets no font. For fonts, sizes and leading, use dualkit-type-pairing.

## Set direction on the document

```tsx
<html lang={locale} dir={locale === 'ar' ? 'rtl' : 'ltr'}>
```

Use the `dir` attribute, never `direction` in CSS. Flex, grid, text alignment and native controls follow it for free. Set `lang` too, because fonts, hyphenation and screen readers depend on it.

## Logical properties, always

| Physical (never) | Logical (always) |
|---|---|
| `margin-left` | `margin-inline-start` |
| `padding-right` | `padding-inline-end` |
| `left: 0` | `inset-inline-start: 0` |
| `text-align: left` | `text-align: start` |
| `border-left` | `border-inline-start` |
| `border-top-left-radius` | `border-start-start-radius` |

`transform: translateX()`, `background-position`, SVG coordinates and animation keyframes do **not** flip. Negate them for right to left:

```css
[dir="rtl"] .x { transform: translateX(100%) }
```

## Left-to-right runs inside Arabic text (the bug every model ships)

In an RTL line, the bidi algorithm resolves the spaces between digit groups to right-to-left, so each group becomes its own run and `مثال 050 123 4567` renders as `مثال 4567 123 050`. The same thing happens to `+971 50 123 4567`, card numbers, times like `10:30 - 11:00`, and versions.

Wrap every such run in an isolate:

```tsx
<p>{t.phoneHint} <bdi dir="ltr">050 123 4567</bdi></p>
```

When markup is not possible (placeholders, `aria-label`, toasts, push text), put the isolate characters in the string: `⁦` (LRI) before the run and `⁩` (PDI) after it.

```ts
const hint = `مثال ⁦050 123 4567⁩`
```

`dir="ltr"` is only for runs with no Arabic letters in them. A formatted Arabic amount (`1,234.50 د.إ`) or an Arabic date contains Arabic, so give it a plain `<bdi>` with no `dir`. Forcing it to LTR moves the currency to the wrong side. The same goes for user-supplied names and values: plain `<bdi>`, so each one picks its own direction.

## Punctuation and brackets

A block with no Arabic in it still runs right to left on an Arabic page, so its last mark moves to the start. `Gregorian:` shows as `:Gregorian`, and `Great service, thank you!` shows as `!Great service, thank you`.

A Latin label in Arabic UI: translate it. If the Latin word must stay, isolate the word and its mark together. An isolate around the word alone leaves the colon at the wrong end.

```html
<label>ميلادي:</label>
<label><bdi>Gregorian:</bdi></label>
```

A bracketed code next to a number, in a cell or badge with no Arabic: `1,234.50 (AED)` shows as `(AED) 1,234.50`. Put the whole amount, brackets included, inside the isolate, or use the Arabic abbreviation.

```html
<td><bdi>1,234.50 (AED)</bdi></td>
<td><bdi>1,234.50 د.إ</bdi></td>
```

User content in either script (reviews, comments): give the block `dir="auto"`, so it takes the direction of its first letter.

```html
<p dir="auto">Great service, thank you!</p>
```

## Inputs: direction by what people type

Fixed formats (phone, IBAN, Emirates ID, card, OTP), email and URL use `dir="ltr"`. Fields where people may type either script (names, search, addresses, free text) use `dir="auto"`. The Dualkit starter Field uses `dir="auto"` on every input, which also works, because a value with no Arabic letters resolves to left to right. Keep the label and the error message in the page direction.

```html
<input type="tel" dir="ltr" inputmode="tel" autocomplete="tel">
<input type="text" dir="auto" autocomplete="name">
```

A `+971` prefix chip sits inside the same `dir="ltr"` wrapper as the input, so it stays on the left.

## What to mirror

| Mirror | Do not mirror |
|---|---|
| Back and forward arrows, chevrons | Play, pause, fast-forward (they follow tape direction) |
| Progress bars, steppers, sliders | Clocks and anything circular that shows time |
| Send icons, reply and redo arrows | Checkmarks, logos, brand marks |
| Carousels and swipe direction | Phone numbers, numerals, Latin text, code |
| Drawers (open from the start edge) | Media timelines |

Flip the icon you already have, never a separate icon set:

```css
[dir="rtl"] .icon-directional { transform: scaleX(-1) }
```

Do not mirror with `rotate(180deg)`. It turns an icon that is not vertically symmetric upside down.

## Common mistakes

- `left: 0` or `margin-left` on anything that should follow the reading edge.
- Mirroring an icon with a 180 degree rotate. Use `scaleX(-1)`.
- A phone number or IBAN example in an Arabic hint or error, with no isolate.
- Forcing `dir="ltr"` on the whole field group, which flips the label too.
- `<bdi dir="ltr">` around a formatted Arabic amount or date. Use `<bdi>` with no `dir`.
- A Latin word leading an Arabic label, like `الgregorian:`. Translate the label.
- A bare `(AED)` next to a number in RTL, with no isolate.
- `text-xs` badges in Arabic. They are 12px, under the 14px floor.
- Testing RTL only with Lorem ipsum. Test with real Arabic copy and real numbers mixed in.

## In Tailwind

Use the logical classes: `ms-4`, `pe-4`, `text-start`, `border-s`, `rounded-ss-lg`, and `inset-s-0` for `inset-inline-start` (v4.2+; `start-0` before, deprecated since). Negate transforms with the `rtl:` variant, as in `rtl:-translate-x-full`. Mirror directional icons with `rtl:-scale-x-100`. In Arabic, `text-sm` (14px) is the smallest size. Never `text-xs` or an arbitrary size under 14px.
